Q: I am trying to write a method that combines two arrays of strings into one new array of strings, but I am blocked with a casting error A: <p>The casting error comes from this line:</p>
<p><code>for (String element : aBag1.toArray())</code></p>
<p>Most bag/collection <code>toArray()</code> methods (if they follow Java’s <code>Collection</code> contract) return an <code>Object[]</code>.<br />
But the enhanced for-loop here expects a <code>String[]</code>. Java won’t auto-cast <code>Object[]</code> to <code>String[]</code>, so you get a <code>ClassCastException</code>.</p>
<p>Use the typed <code>toArray(T[])</code> overload (best if available)</p>
<p>If your <code>BagInterface</code> provides a <code>toArray(T[] dest)</code> similar to <code>Collection.toArray(T[])</code>, use it to get a <code>String[]</code> safely:</p>
<pre><code>static BagInterface&lt;String&gt; createCombineBag(BagInterface&lt;String&gt; aBag1, BagInterface&lt;String&gt; aBag2) {
    if (aBag1 == null || aBag2 == null) {
        return null;
    }

    BagInterface&lt;String&gt; newBag = new LinkedBag&lt;&gt;();

    // Get type-safe arrays
    String[] arr1 = aBag1.toArray(new String[0]);
    String[] arr2 = aBag2.toArray(new String[0]);

    for (String element : arr1) {
        newBag.add(element);
    }
    for (String element : arr2) {
        newBag.add(element);
    }

    return newBag;
}
</code></pre>
